Remove what ZTARC does not have, and add what AGPL-3 requires.

Structural changes a string substitution cannot make:

  * "Pangolin Cloud" — there is no ZTARC hosted service. The button is hidden
    in updateUI rather than deleted, so the state machine around it is
    untouched and an upstream rewrite of that machine still applies cleanly.
    Its label survives in the binary as an unreachable string; that is the
    price of not restructuring code we do not own.
  * An empty server field — the console is the only way in, so it is prefilled
    from config.DefaultHostname.
  * Terms of Service and Privacy Policy — in the login window, the About tab and
    the tray menu. ztarc.io has no such pages, and a link that goes nowhere is
    worse than no link. Their declarations go with them, or Go rejects the
    unused variables.
  * "Install CLI" — the binary behind it is the upstream vendor's, fetched from
    their GitHub releases. Renaming the menu entry would have been the dishonest
    fix; it stays hidden until ZTARC has a CLI of its own.

And one addition, in both places the removed links occupied. The About tab's
Legal section and the tray's More menu now carry "Source code (AGPL-3)". This
client is a modified build of AGPL-3 software, so everyone who runs it is
entitled to the corresponding source; a link where they will actually look is
the honest way to offer it.

Applied by scripts/rebrand.sh before the sed rules, so the context lines here
match pristine upstream. If fosrl rewrites either file this fails loudly, which
is the intent.

Note how this file is applied: from the repository root with
`git apply --directory=build/src`, never `git -C build/src apply`. build/src is
inside this work tree, so the latter resolves paths against the repository root,
finds nothing to patch, and exits 0 — which is exactly what it did, silently,
for every build until scripts/audit-brand.sh started asserting the results.

